column2:
Do not free me, illustrious one, knowing that relationships are impermanent; birds are food for hunters, and life is the food of life.
brahmapuran:80:47	I do not remember his offence; make your mind firm in dharma. Fire is the teacher of the twice-born, and the brahmin the teacher of the classes;
brahmapuran:80:48	the husband alone is the teacher of women, and the guest is the teacher of all. Those who please a guest who has come with their words —
brahmapuran:80:49	the goddess of speech is surely satisfied with them; by giving him food, Shakra is satisfied;
brahmapuran:80:50	by washing his feet the ancestors, by food and the like Prajapati; by serving him Lakshmi with Vishnu is pleased;
brahmapuran:80:51	by giving him a bed all the gods; therefore the guest is most worthy of honour. A guest who is weary, who has come to the house at sunset, should be known as a god in form, for he brings the fruit of all sacrifices.
brahmapuran:80:52	All the gods, ancestors and fires follow a weary guest; when he is satisfied they rejoice, and when he goes away disappointed, they too are disappointed.
brahmapuran:80:53	Therefore, beloved, with all your being give up grief and become calm; keep a good mind and do the work of dharma.
brahmapuran:80:54	Help and harm are both considered excellent; everyone helps those who help them in return,
brahmapuran:80:55	but the good man who helps those who harm him is declared to partake of merit.
